{"title":"Finite Automata","summary":"A finite automaton is an abstract machine with a finite set of states that reads an input string symbol by symbol, moving between states according to a transition function, and accepts or rejects based on whether it ends in an accepting state — formally a 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F): states Q, input alphabet Σ, transition function δ, start state q₀, and accepting states F. A Deterministic Finite Automaton (DFA) has exactly one transition per state per symbol (δ: Q×Σ→Q), so its behavior is completely determined; a Nondeterministic Finite Automaton (NFA) allows zero, one, or multiple transitions per state per symbol (including ε-transitions consuming no input), accepting if at least one possible path ends in an accepting state. Despite looking more powerful, every NFA has an equivalent DFA accepting the same language, constructed via subset construction: each DFA state represents a set of NFA states that could be active simultaneously, meaning the DFA can have up to 2ⁿ states for an n-state NFA in the worst case, even though NFAs and DFAs recognize exactly the same class of languages (the regular languages). This makes finite automata the right tool whenever a problem needs only a bounded, finite amount of memory (which state you're in) regardless of input length, unlike problems requiring counting or matching nested structure, which need more powerful models. A minimal DFA (fewest states recognizing a given language) is unique up to renaming, and minimization algorithms exist specifically to find this canonical, smallest representation.","learning_outcomes":["Given a DFA's formal definition or state diagram, trace its execution on a given input string and determine whether it's accepted or rejected.","Convert a given NFA (including one with ε-transitions) into an equivalent DFA using the subset construction, and verify the resulting DFA accepts the same language on a sample input.","Design a DFA or NFA that accepts a language described by a specific property, and justify what each state represents."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming an NFA can accept strings a DFA cannot — NFAs and DFAs recognize exactly the same class of languages; NFAs are only more convenient to design for some languages, not more expressive.","Forgetting to include a 'trap' or 'dead' state when converting an incomplete transition function into a proper DFA — a DFA's transition function must be total, so missing transitions need an explicit non-accepting sink state.","Confusing 'the NFA has multiple transitions on a symbol' with 'it must satisfy all branches' — an NFA accepts if at least one path leads to acceptance, not if every path does.","Assuming subset construction always produces exactly 2ⁿ states — that's only the worst-case upper bound; many NFAs convert to DFAs with far fewer states, since only reachable, distinct subsets become DFA states."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Convert an NFA with states {q0,q1,q2}, start q0, accepting {q2}, and transitions δ(q0,0)={q0,q1}, δ(q0,1)={q0}, δ(q1,1)={q2} (all others empty) into an equivalent DFA via subset construction.","steps":["Start DFA state: {q0}.","From {q0}: on 0, δ(q0,0)={q0,q1} (new state); on 1, δ(q0,1)={q0} (self-loop).","From {q0,q1}: on 0, union gives {q0,q1}; on 1, union of δ(q0,1)∪δ(q1,1)={q0}∪{q2}={q0,q2} (new state).","From {q0,q2}: on 0, union gives {q0,q1} (existing); on 1, union gives {q0} (existing) — no new states generated.","Mark states containing q2 as accepting: {q0,q2} is the sole accepting DFA state."],"result":"Subset construction produces a 3-state DFA ({q0}, {q0,q1}, {q0,q2}) — far fewer than the worst-case 2³=8 — accepting exactly the strings ending in '01', confirmed by tracing sample strings like '01' and '0101' (reach {q0,q2}, accepted) versus '010' (reaches {q0,q1}, rejected)."}}
{"title":"Regular Languages & Regular Expressions","summary":"A regular language is any language describable by a regular expression, built from single characters and the empty string using concatenation (AB matches A's string followed by B's), union/alternation (A|B matches either), and Kleene star (A* matches zero or more repetitions) — Kleene's theorem establishes that regular expressions, DFAs, and NFAs all describe exactly the same class of languages, so any can be mechanically converted into either other (Thompson's construction converts a regex directly into an equivalent NFA, piece by piece). Regular languages are closed under union, concatenation, Kleene star, intersection, and complement, meaning combining regular languages with these operations always produces another regular language — this closure lets complex pattern-matching be built compositionally from simpler regular pieces. Not every language is regular, and the pumping lemma is the standard tool for proving a language is not regular: for any regular language L, there is a pumping length p such that any string in L of length at least p can be split into three parts xyz, where |xy|≤p, |y|≥1, and xyⁱz is also in L for every i≥0 — the classic use is showing {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n≥0} is not regular, since any candidate split forces y (constrained to lie within the first p characters, all 0s) to be pumped, breaking the equal-count requirement. This limitation exists because a finite automaton has no way to count an unbounded quantity — with only finitely many states, it cannot remember 'how many 0s so far' once that count could exceed the state count, which is exactly why more powerful models (context-free grammars, with a stack) are needed for balanced or nested-counting requirements.","learning_outcomes":["Given a regular expression, construct an equivalent NFA using Thompson's construction (or describe the language it matches in plain English).","Apply the pumping lemma to prove that a given language (such as {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n≥0}) is not regular, correctly identifying the required split and the pumping violation.","Use the closure properties of regular languages to argue that a language built from known regular languages is itself regular."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a language that 'looks simple' must be regular — many simple-looking languages (balanced parentheses, equal counts of two symbols) are provably not regular precisely because they require unbounded counting.","Misapplying the pumping lemma by choosing a split that doesn't force a contradiction — the proof requires showing EVERY possible valid split leads to a pumped string outside the language, not just exhibiting one convenient split.","Forgetting the constraint |xy|≤p when choosing what to pump — this constraint forces y into a specific, often-predictable region of the string, and ignoring it can make an invalid proof attempt seem to work.","Believing regular languages are closed under every conceivable operation — closure holds for the specific proven operations (union, concatenation, Kleene star, intersection, complement), not as a blanket guarantee for anything you might do to a regular language."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Use the pumping lemma to prove L = {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n≥0} is not regular.","steps":["Assume L is regular; the pumping lemma guarantees a pumping length p.","Choose s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ, which is in L and has length 2p≥p.","By the lemma, s=xyz with |xy|≤p, |y|≥1, and xyⁱz∈L for all i≥0. Since |xy|≤p and the first p characters are all 0s, x and y consist entirely of 0s.","Consider i=2: xy²z has p+|y| zeros but still exactly p ones (y contains no 1s)."],"result":"Since |y|≥1, xy²z has more 0s than 1s, so it's not in L — contradicting the lemma's guarantee that xyⁱz∈L for all i, so L cannot be regular, because no finite automaton can track an unbounded, exactly-matching count of 0s and 1s."}}
{"title":"Context-Free Grammars & Pushdown Automata","summary":"A context-free grammar (CFG) generates a language through production rules A→α, where A is a single non-terminal and α is a string of terminals and non-terminals — 'context-free' refers to a non-terminal always being replaceable by its production regardless of what surrounds it. A derivation starts from a start symbol and repeatedly applies productions until only terminals remain; the sequence forms a parse tree, and a grammar is ambiguous if some string has two or more distinct parse trees, which matters because ambiguous grammars (like a naive arithmetic grammar without precedence) can assign different meanings to the same input, exactly what operator-precedence rules solve in real language grammars. A pushdown automaton (PDA) is a finite automaton augmented with a stack — unbounded auxiliary memory accessible only last-in-first-out — and this single extra piece of memory is exactly what lets a PDA recognize non-regular languages like {0ⁿ1ⁿ : n≥0} (push a symbol per 0, pop one per 1, accept only if the stack empties exactly when input ends). Context-free grammars and pushdown automata are equivalent in expressive power — every CFG has an equivalent PDA and vice versa — so context-free languages are exactly what a PDA recognizes, a strictly larger class than the regular languages (every regular language is context-free, since a DFA is a PDA that never uses its stack, but {0ⁿ1ⁿ} is context-free without being regular). Context-free grammars are the standard tool for specifying a programming language's syntax, and a compiler's parser is, at its core, an implementation of a pushdown automaton checking whether a token stream is derivable from the grammar.","learning_outcomes":["Given a context-free grammar, derive a string in its language by applying production rules step by step, and construct the corresponding parse tree.","Determine whether a given grammar is ambiguous by finding (or failing to find) two distinct parse trees for the same string.","Design a pushdown automaton (or trace a given one) that recognizes a simple non-regular language, explaining how the stack enforces the counting requirement."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming every context-free grammar is unambiguous by default — ambiguity must be checked or proven absent; many naturally-written grammars (especially arithmetic without precedence) are ambiguous unless specifically designed not to be.","Confusing a derivation with a parse tree — different derivation orders (leftmost vs rightmost) can still correspond to the same tree, since the tree captures only hierarchical structure, not the order rules were applied.","Assuming a PDA's stack can be read arbitrarily like general memory — a stack only allows access to its top element, so a PDA can only use what's currently on top to decide its next move.","Believing all context-free languages are also regular, or vice versa — context-free languages strictly contain the regular languages, but languages like {0ⁿ1ⁿ} are context-free without being regular, since they need the stack's unbounded counting."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Trace a pushdown automaton accepting balanced parentheses (push '(' on '(', pop on ')', accept if the stack is empty exactly when input ends) on \"(()())\".","steps":["Read '(' (pos1): push. Stack: [(].","Read '(' (pos2): push. Stack: [(, (].","Read ')' (pos3): pop. Stack: [(].","Read '(' (pos4): push. Stack: [(, (].","Read ')' (pos5): pop. Stack: [(].","Read ')' (pos6): pop. Stack: [] (empty)."],"result":"After all 6 characters, the stack is empty exactly when input ends, so \"(()())\" is accepted — stack depth (1,2,1,2,1,0) exactly tracks nesting depth, the unbounded counting ability a finite automaton lacks, which is why balanced parentheses is context-free but not regular."}}
{"title":"Turing Machines & Computability","summary":"A Turing machine is the most general model of computation studied in theory: it has an infinite tape (unlimited read/write memory), a head reading/writing symbols and moving left or right one cell at a time, and a finite set of states governed by δ(state, symbol)→(new state, symbol to write, direction) — unlike a finite automaton or pushdown automaton, its tape gives unbounded, freely-accessible memory, not just states or a stack. The Church-Turing thesis (a widely accepted claim, not a provable theorem) states that any function computable by any reasonable, physically realizable model (including every real programming language and computer, memory limits aside) is computable by some Turing machine, making it the standard reference point for defining 'computable'. A language is decidable if some Turing machine halts on every input, always correctly accepting or rejecting; it's undecidable if no such always-halting machine exists, even though a machine might still correctly accept every string that should be accepted while looping forever on some that shouldn't (making it merely 'recognizable', a strictly weaker guarantee). The most famous undecidable problem is the halting problem — given a program and input, determine whether it halts — proved unsolvable by Turing via diagonalization: assuming a hypothetical halts-checker H existed, one can construct a program that calls H on itself and does the opposite of what H predicts, producing a direct contradiction when run on a description of itself, showing H cannot exist. Undecidability isn't abstract: many practical questions (whether code always terminates, whether two programs are exactly equivalent) reduce to the halting problem or similarly undecidable problems, which is why static analysis tools can only offer partial guarantees rather than a complete, always-correct answer.","learning_outcomes":["Given a Turing machine's transition function or state diagram, trace its execution on a small input tape, including head movement and tape modifications, until it halts or loops.","Explain the distinction between a decidable language and a recognizable (but possibly undecidable) language, in terms of what guarantee the corresponding Turing machine does or doesn't provide.","Explain, at the level of the diagonalization argument's core idea, why the halting problem is undecidable, including what contradiction arises from assuming a halts-checker exists."],"common_mistakes":["Treating the Church-Turing thesis as a proven mathematical theorem — it cannot be formally proven since 'any reasonable computational model' isn't a rigorously defined object, though no counterexample has ever been found.","Confusing 'undecidable' with 'we haven't found an algorithm yet' — undecidability is a proven impossibility result, not a statement about current limits of technique or computing power.","Assuming a Turing machine that doesn't halt on a specific input means the language is undecidable — a language can be decidable even if one particular machine for it fails to halt; undecidability is about whether ANY correct, always-halting decider can exist.","Believing every practical termination question is unanswerable because the general halting problem is undecidable — many specific programs or restricted classes (no loops, provably bounded loops) can still be analyzed correctly; undecidability is about the general case, not every individual instance."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Walk through the halting problem's diagonalization proof by assuming a halts-checker H exists and deriving a contradiction.","steps":["Assume H(P,I) correctly outputs 'halts' or 'loops' for any program P on input I, always halting itself.","Construct D(P): runs H(P,P); if H says 'halts', D loops forever; if H says 'loops', D halts immediately.","Run D on its own description: D(D), which calls H(D,D).","Case A: H(D,D) says 'halts' — by D's construction, D(D) then loops forever, contradicting H's report.","Case B: H(D,D) says 'loops' — by D's construction, D(D) then halts immediately, contradicting H's report."],"result":"Both cases produce a direct contradiction between what H predicts and what D(D) actually does, so the assumption that a correct, always-halting H exists must be false — no algorithm can solve the halting problem in general."}}
{"title":"Complexity Theory (P vs NP)","summary":"Complexity theory classifies decision problems by the resources needed to solve them as a function of input size; P is the set of problems solvable by a deterministic algorithm in polynomial time, while NP is the set of problems whose proposed solution (a 'certificate') can be verified in polynomial time, even if finding that solution takes much longer — every P problem is also in NP (if you can solve it quickly, you can trivially verify a proposed solution by just solving it yourself), but whether P equals NP is the most famous open problem in computer science, unresolved despite decades of effort and a $1 million Clay Millennium Prize for a proof either way. A problem is NP-hard if every NP problem reduces to it in polynomial time, and NP-complete if it is both NP-hard and itself in NP — the Cook-Levin theorem (1971) was the first proof that a specific problem, Boolean satisfiability (SAT: does a formula have a variable assignment making it true?), is NP-complete, and since then thousands of other problems (including traveling salesman, graph coloring, and knapsack, in their decision-problem forms) have been proven NP-complete by reduction from SAT or from each other. A polynomial-time reduction from problem A to problem B shows B is 'at least as hard' as A, since any fast algorithm for B, plus the polynomial transformation, could solve A fast too — this is the core technique for proving new problems NP-complete, since proving one problem NP-complete from first principles and then chaining reductions is far easier than proving each new problem independently. If a problem is proven NP-complete, this is strong evidence (though not proof) that no polynomial-time algorithm exists for it, since a polynomial algorithm for any single NP-complete problem would imply P=NP and solve every NP problem efficiently — which is why NP-complete problems are typically tackled with heuristics, approximation algorithms, or algorithms fast on typical (not worst-case) inputs, rather than waiting for an exact polynomial solution.","learning_outcomes":["Given a decision problem, determine whether it's plausibly in P or in NP, and explain the certificate for an NP problem.","Explain the relationship between NP-hard and NP-complete, and what it means for the Cook-Levin theorem to establish SAT as the first known NP-complete problem.","Given a polynomial-time reduction from a known NP-complete problem to a new problem, explain why this proves the new problem is NP-hard."],"common_mistakes":["Believing NP stands for 'not polynomial' — it stands for 'nondeterministic polynomial time' (equivalently, polynomial-time verifiable); P is a subset of NP, so every P problem is also in NP.","Treating 'NP-complete' as proof that no polynomial-time algorithm exists — it's strong evidence (solving any single NP-complete problem in polynomial time would prove P=NP), but P≠NP remains an unproven conjecture.","Confusing NP-hard with NP-complete — every NP-complete problem is NP-hard, but not every NP-hard problem is in NP; NP-complete requires both properties.","Assuming a problem proven NP-complete is therefore unsolvable in practice — approximation algorithms, heuristics, and algorithms fast on typical inputs are routinely used to handle NP-complete problems effectively for real-world instances."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Given φ = (x₁ ∨ ¬x₂) ∧ (¬x₁ ∨ x₂ ∨ x₃) ∧ (¬x₃), verify whether the certificate x₁=True, x₂=True, x₃=False satisfies φ.","steps":["Clause 1: (x₁ ∨ ¬x₂) = (True ∨ ¬True) = (True ∨ False) = True.","Clause 2: (¬x₁ ∨ x₂ ∨ x₃) = (False ∨ True ∨ False) = True.","Clause 3: (¬x₃) = ¬False = True.","Combine with AND: True ∧ True ∧ True = True."],"result":"The certificate satisfies φ, confirmed in just 3 clause evaluations regardless of how hard the assignment was to find — illustrating why SAT is in NP: verifying a proposed solution is fast even though finding one can require exponential search in the worst case."}}
{"title":"Lexical Analysis","summary":"Lexical analysis (scanning) is the compiler's first phase, converting a raw stream of source characters into a sequence of tokens — each token a categorized unit like an identifier, keyword, number, or operator, represented as a (token-type, lexeme) pair, where the lexeme is the actual matched text. This phase directly applies regular languages and finite automata: each token category is defined by a regular expression, and the lexer itself is essentially a DFA that consumes characters and recognizes which regex matched, which is why lexer-generator tools compile a set of regexes directly into a single, efficient DFA. When multiple patterns could match at the current position, lexers apply maximal munch (longest match) — always preferring the longest lexeme — which is why 'foreach' tokenizes as one IDENTIFIER rather than 'for' followed by 'each', and ties between equal-length matches are broken by rule priority (keywords checked before the more general identifier pattern, so 'if' matches the IF keyword rather than a generic identifier). The lexer also strips whitespace and comments and begins populating the symbol table with identifiers it encounters, though full semantic information (like type) is typically filled in during later phases. Lexical errors occur when input matches no valid token pattern (an unterminated string, an illegal character), and because a lexer processes input in a single left-to-right pass without backtracking into already-tokenized text, good error recovery (skipping to the next likely token boundary) matters for reporting multiple errors in one pass rather than stopping at the first.","learning_outcomes":["Given a set of token patterns (as regular expressions) and a source snippet, manually tokenize it into (token-type, lexeme) pairs, applying maximal munch where patterns could otherwise overlap.","Explain how a lexer's use of regular expressions and DFAs connects to the theoretical equivalence between regular expressions and finite automata.","Identify a lexical error in a given source snippet and explain why it's caught at the lexical phase rather than later."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a lexer needs to understand a program's meaning to tokenize it — lexical analysis is purely pattern-based and has no awareness of syntax or semantics.","Forgetting maximal munch and assuming a lexer splits 'foreach' into 'for' and 'each' — a correct lexer always prefers the longest valid match, so 'foreach' becomes one IDENTIFIER (unless itself a reserved keyword).","Assuming keyword recognition happens as a separate phase from identifier recognition — keywords are just identifiers matching a reserved word, checked via priority during the same tokenization pass.","Believing all input errors are caught during lexical analysis — a lexer only catches character-to-token pattern errors; errors in token arrangement (a missing semicolon) are syntax errors caught during parsing."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Tokenize the source snippet 'if(count>=10)' into (token-type, lexeme) pairs, applying maximal munch and keyword-vs-identifier priority.","steps":["'if' matches both KEYWORD and IDENTIFIER patterns; keyword priority wins: token = KEYWORD('if').","'(' matches only LPAREN: token = LPAREN('(').","'count' matches IDENTIFIER; maximal munch consumes all 5 characters: token = IDENTIFIER('count').","'>' could tokenize alone as GT, but '=' immediately follows forming the longer match '>='; maximal munch prefers it: token = GE('>=').","'10' matches NUMBER; maximal munch consumes both digits: token = NUMBER('10').","')' matches only RPAREN: token = RPAREN(')')."],"result":"Token sequence: KEYWORD('if'), LPAREN('('), IDENTIFIER('count'), GE('>='), NUMBER('10'), RPAREN(')') — six tokens from thirteen characters, with maximal munch correctly merging 'count', '>=', and '10' into single tokens rather than splitting them."}}
{"title":"Parsing (Syntax Analysis)","summary":"Parsing (syntax analysis) takes the lexer's token stream and determines whether it conforms to the language's grammar, building a parse tree (or more commonly an Abstract Syntax Tree, AST, which omits structural details like explicit parenthesis tokens and keeps only semantically meaningful structure) — this directly applies context-free grammars and pushdown automata, since a parser essentially implements a PDA checking whether the token stream is derivable from the grammar's start symbol. Top-down parsing builds the tree from the root down to the leaves, predicting which production to apply based on upcoming tokens (recursive-descent, often hand-written, and LL parsers using a lookup table); bottom-up parsing starts from the leaves and works up, repeatedly reducing a production's right-hand side to its non-terminal (LR parsers, typically generator-produced, handling a strictly larger class of grammars than LL). A key practical challenge is grammar ambiguity, especially for expressions: a naive grammar like E → E+E | E*E | number doesn't specify whether '2+3*4' parses as (2+3)*4 or 2+(3*4), so real grammars are restructured into separate precedence levels (a distinct non-terminal per precedence tier) so the grammar itself enforces that multiplication binds tighter than addition, and similarly separate rules enforce left- versus right-associativity for operators like subtraction versus exponentiation. A syntax error occurs when the token stream cannot be derived from the grammar at all (a missing closing parenthesis, two operators with no operand between), and unlike a lexical error, it's about the arrangement of otherwise-valid tokens rather than an invalid character sequence. The AST is the structural backbone every later phase (semantic analysis, code generation) operates on, which is why a correct, unambiguous grammar is foundational to everything downstream.","learning_outcomes":["Given a simple context-free grammar and a token stream, construct a parse tree using either a top-down or bottom-up approach, showing which production is applied at each step.","Explain why a naive arithmetic expression grammar is ambiguous, and restructure it into separate precedence-level non-terminals to resolve the ambiguity.","Distinguish a syntax error from a lexical error given an example, and explain which compiler phase catches each."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a parse tree and an AST are the same thing — a parse tree includes every grammar symbol used, while an AST is simplified, keeping only semantically meaningful structure.","Believing any grammar restructuring that removes ambiguity is acceptable — the restructured grammar must still generate exactly the same language as the original, just with a unique parse tree per string.","Assuming LL and LR parsers can handle exactly the same grammars — LR handles a strictly larger class, including some left-recursive grammars that cause naive top-down parsers to loop infinitely without modification.","Treating any token-arrangement problem as a lexical error — a syntax error involves individually valid tokens arranged in a way the grammar doesn't permit, caught during parsing, not the lexical phase that already tokenized each piece successfully."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Given the ambiguous grammar E → E+E | E*E | number, restructure it as E → E+T | T; T → T*F | F; F → number, then parse '2+3*4' to confirm it resolves as 2+(3*4).","steps":["Restructure into precedence levels: E handles addition (lowest precedence); T handles multiplication (higher precedence, one level below E); F is the base case — multiplication is only reachable through T, guaranteeing it binds tighter.","Parse '2+3*4' top-down: apply E→E+T, splitting into an E part (before '+') and a T part (after '+').","The left E reduces via T→F→'2', so the left side is just 2.","The right T must account for '3*4'; apply T→T*F, splitting into a T part ('3') and an F part ('4')."],"result":"'3*4' is grouped together as a single T subtree beneath the '+', so the tree structurally represents 2+(3*4) — because the grammar only allows E→E+T (never a flat E+E+E), '*' is forced to bind within a T one level below '+', resolving the original ambiguity in favor of standard operator precedence."}}
{"title":"Semantic Analysis & Type Checking","summary":"Semantic analysis checks that a syntactically valid program actually makes sense according to the language's rules — a program can parse successfully while still being semantically invalid, such as using an undeclared variable, or adding a string to a boolean in a language that doesn't permit it. The symbol table, first populated during lexical analysis, is filled in fully here with each identifier's type, scope, and other attributes, and is used to resolve every identifier reference to its declaration — checking that a variable is declared before use (and only used within its declared scope) is one of the most fundamental semantic checks, since the grammar alone can't express 'this name must have been declared earlier'. Type checking is the other central task: for statically-typed languages, the compiler verifies every operation is applied to compatible types and that function calls supply arguments matching declared parameter types, entirely before the program runs — this catches an entire category of bugs at compile time rather than as a runtime crash, the central trade-off against dynamically-typed languages, where the equivalent checks happen at runtime instead. Some type mismatches are resolved automatically through type coercion (implicit conversion, like promoting an integer to floating-point in mixed-type arithmetic), while others are flagged as errors requiring explicit conversion or a fix, and the specific rules for what coerces automatically versus what's a hard error are entirely language-defined, not universal. Because semantic analysis operates on the AST built during parsing (via a tree traversal annotating each node with its resolved type and checking it against context), this phase is where the compiler's understanding of 'what the program means' is built, forming the foundation for code generation.","learning_outcomes":["Given a short code snippet, identify semantic errors (undeclared variable use, out-of-scope reference, type mismatch) that would pass lexical and syntax analysis but fail semantic analysis.","Trace how a symbol table is populated and queried to resolve variable declarations and check scope rules for a small program with nested scopes.","Given a statically-typed language's type rules, determine whether a specific expression or function call type-checks, including where implicit type coercion would apply."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming a program that parses successfully is guaranteed to run — a program can be grammatically well-formed while still referencing an undeclared variable or mismatching types, both semantic errors caught in a later phase.","Confusing type coercion (an automatic, language-defined implicit conversion) with a type error — not every mismatch is invalid; some are silently resolved by coercion rules specific to the language, others require explicit conversion.","Assuming scope checking only asks whether a name was declared 'somewhere' — a variable must be declared in a scope actually visible at the point of use, respecting nested scope and shadowing rules.","Believing dynamically-typed languages skip type checking entirely — they still check types, just at runtime, so an error a static compiler would catch before execution instead surfaces as a runtime crash."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Given: int x=5; then a block { string x=\"hello\"; print(x); } then print(x) again — trace how semantic analysis resolves each use of 'x' via scope rules.","steps":["The outer scope declares x with type int, value 5; symbol table records (name=x, type=int, scope=outer).","Entering the inner block creates a new nested scope; declaring string x=\"hello\" adds (name=x, type=string, scope=inner), which shadows the outer x for the rest of the inner scope.","The first print(x) occurs inside the inner scope, so it resolves to the innermost matching declaration — the inner string x.","Exiting the block discards the inner scope's entries; the second print(x) occurs back in the outer scope, where only the outer int x is visible."],"result":"The two print(x) calls resolve to different declarations — the first to the inner string x ('hello'), the second to the outer int x (5) — semantic analysis correctly distinguishes them purely through scope-aware symbol table resolution, despite the identical name."}}
{"title":"Intermediate Code & Optimization","summary":"After semantic analysis validates the AST, many compilers translate it into an intermediate representation (IR) — lower-level than the source language but independent of any target machine — before generating final machine code; this separation lets a compiler with n source languages and m targets need only n front-ends and m back-ends rather than n×m direct translators, and it's where most optimization happens, since optimizing the IR once benefits every target. A common teaching IR is three-address code, where each instruction has at most one operator and up to three operands (e.g. 't1 = b * c'); a sequence of three-address instructions with no internal jumps forms a basic block, and a function's basic blocks connected by control-flow edges form its control-flow graph (CFG), the structure most optimization algorithms operate over. Constant folding is the simplest classic optimization: an expression whose operands are all compile-time constants (like '2*3+1') is evaluated once during compilation and replaced with its result ('7'), rather than recomputed every run. Dead code elimination removes computations whose results are never used (a variable assigned but never read, or code after an unconditional return), while common subexpression elimination detects when the same expression is computed multiple times with no changes to its operands in between and reuses a single computed value — all three preserve the program's observable behavior exactly while reducing runtime work. Because an IR exposes program structure in a simplified, uniform way, it's the natural place for optimizations that are broadly applicable and provably behavior-preserving, which is why real compilers apply dozens of these passes, often repeatedly, before generating target-specific machine code.","learning_outcomes":["Translate a simple arithmetic or control-flow source statement into three-address code, and identify the basic blocks and control-flow graph structure it forms.","Apply constant folding to a given expression or code snippet, identifying which sub-expressions can be evaluated at compile time.","Identify opportunities for dead code elimination and common subexpression elimination in a given short piece of intermediate code, and show the optimized result."],"common_mistakes":["Applying common subexpression elimination without checking that operands genuinely haven't changed between computations — if a variable was reassigned in between, reusing the earlier value silently produces an incorrect result.","Assuming dead code elimination can remove any code that 'looks unused' without analyzing actual control and data flow — code that appears unreachable might still be reachable via a jump or loop.","Believing intermediate representations exist only for optimization — the n-front-ends/m-back-ends architectural benefit is a distinct, equally important reason IRs exist, independent of any optimization passes.","Assuming optimizations are applied in a single fixed pass — real compilers apply many passes repeatedly, since one optimization can expose new opportunities for another that weren't visible before."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Optimize this three-address code (only 'y' is used after this block; a, b are runtime variables, not compile-time constants): t1=2*3; t2=a+b; t3=a+b; t4=t1+t3; unused=a*100; y=t4.","steps":["Constant folding: 't1 = 2*3' has both operands as compile-time constants, evaluated once and replaced with 't1 = 6'.","Common subexpression elimination: 't3 = a+b' recomputes the same expression as 't2 = a+b' with a,b unchanged in between, so t3 is replaced by reusing t2.","Dead code elimination: 'unused = a*100' is never read afterward (only y is used later), so this instruction is removed entirely.","After folding and CSE, 't4 = t1+t3' becomes 't4 = 6+t2' — this can't fold further since t2 depends on runtime values of a,b."],"result":"Optimized code: t1=6; t2=a+b; t4=6+t2; y=t4 — four instructions instead of six, with the redundant addition and unused computation eliminated and one multiplication resolved at compile time, while producing exactly the same value for y as the original (6+(a+b) in both cases)."}}
{"title":"Code Generation & Linking","summary":"Code generation is the compiler's final phase, translating the (by now optimized) intermediate representation into actual target-machine instructions — this involves instruction selection (choosing which machine instructions implement each IR operation, which varies between architectures with different instruction sets) and register allocation (deciding which of the limited physical CPU registers holds each program value at each point, since a typical program has far more live variables than a CPU has registers). Register allocation is commonly modeled as a graph coloring problem: build an interference graph where each node is a variable and an edge connects two variables simultaneously live at some point, then try to color the graph with k colors (k = available registers) so no two connected nodes share a color — if no valid k-coloring exists, some variable must be 'spilled' to memory instead, trading a slower memory access for a freed register. Once machine code is generated per source file (a compilation unit) separately, the resulting object file contains the machine instructions plus a symbol table of definitions it provides and unresolved references it expects from elsewhere — the linker combines multiple object files (and libraries) into a single executable, resolving each unresolved reference to its actual address via a process called relocation, since instructions referencing an address need it patched in only once the combined program's final layout is known. Static linking copies all needed library code directly into the executable at link time, producing a larger but self-contained file, while dynamic linking instead records a reference to a shared library loaded and linked at startup (or later, on first use), trading a smaller executable and shared memory usage across programs against a runtime dependency on the library actually being present and version-compatible. Because code generation and linking sit at the very end of the pipeline, an error surfacing here (an undefined reference the linker can't resolve) usually traces back to a missing implementation, missing library, or mismatched declaration, rather than a syntax or semantic problem, which is why linker errors look and feel different from earlier compiler errors.","learning_outcomes":["Explain the difference between instruction selection and register allocation as two distinct sub-tasks of code generation, and why both are necessary.","Given a small set of variables and their live ranges, construct an interference graph and attempt a graph coloring with a specified number of registers, identifying any variable that must be spilled.","Distinguish static linking from dynamic linking in terms of executable size, startup behavior, and runtime dependencies, and identify what an undefined-reference linker error most likely indicates is missing."],"common_mistakes":["Assuming code generation is a simple, one-to-one mechanical translation — instruction selection must account for real architectural differences, so the same IR can compile to very different instruction sequences on different targets.","Assuming every variable can always be kept in a register — if the interference graph can't be colored with the available registers, at least one variable must be spilled to memory, a correct but slower fallback.","Confusing a linker error with a compiler error — a linker error means the code compiled successfully but a referenced definition couldn't be found among the provided object files and libraries, a different failure than a syntax or type error.","Assuming dynamic linking has no downsides compared to static linking — it saves executable size and shares memory across programs, but introduces a runtime dependency: if the required shared library isn't present, the program can fail to start entirely."],"worked_example":{"setup":"Four variables a,b,c,d have live ranges such that: a interferes with b and c; b interferes with a and d; c interferes with a and d; d interferes with b and c. Attempt to color this interference graph with 2 registers.","steps":["Build the interference graph: edges a-b, a-c, b-d, c-d — this forms a 4-cycle (a-b-d-c-a).","Attempt a 2-coloring with registers R1, R2: assign a=R1.","Since a interferes with b and c, both must differ from a: assign b=R2, c=R2.","Since d interferes with b and c (both R2), d must differ from R2: assign d=R1 — safe, since d doesn't interfere with a.","Verify every interfering pair differs: a(R1)-b(R2) ✓, a(R1)-c(R2) ✓, b(R2)-d(R1) ✓, c(R2)-d(R1) ✓."],"result":"A valid 2-coloring exists (a=R1, b=R2, c=R2, d=R1), so all four variables fit in 2 registers with no spilling — because the interference graph forms an even cycle, always 2-colorable; an odd cycle (like a triangle) would instead force at least 3 colors, requiring a spill if only 2 registers were available."}}
